1
00:00:09,890 --> 00:00:14,010
所以，嗯，我很高兴能受邀参加乔什的婚礼。
So, uh, I was thrilled to be invited to Josh's wedding.

2
00:00:14,930 --> 00:00:24,830
我很兴奋看到乔什结婚，但我也同样期待这个机会能
和我的伴郎朋友们聚一聚，也就是我们这群高中同学。
I was excited to see Josh get married, but I was equally excited for this chance
to hang out with my fellow groomsmen, which were the high school friend group.

3
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:31,920
自从丹开始在 OpenAI 工作后，我们都没见过他本人。
None of us had seen Dan in person since he had started working at OpenAI.

4
00:00:32,334 --> 00:00:32,800
我们要开录了吗？
Are we on?

5
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:33,240
是的。
Yes.

6
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:33,860
噢，抱歉。
Oh, sorry.

7
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:36,420
你能把墨镜摘下来吗？
Can you take the sunglasses off?

8
00:00:52,890 --> 00:00:58,010
在成长过程中，我一直在阅读和写作方面挣扎，而且我觉得数学很无聊也没意义。
Growing up, I really struggled reading and writing, and I thought math was boring and pointless.

9
00:00:58,318 --> 00:01:03,190
对我来说真正重要的一件事就是和朋友在一起，并和他们一起创作艺术。
The one thing that really mattered to me was being with my friends and making art with them.

10
00:01:04,878 --> 00:01:10,310
我们一起创作各种音乐，经常聚在一起看电影、开玩笑。
We made all sorts of music together and would hang out all the time and see movies and joke.

11
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:16,900
丹是我最老的朋友了。
Dan is my oldest friend.

12
00:01:18,478 --> 00:01:22,160
到我三四岁的时候，我们就已经形影不离了。
By the time I was three or four years old, we were already inseparable.

13
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:24,600
我们
We

14
00:01:24,600 --> 00:01:27,020
算是一对奇特的组合。
were sort of an odd couple.

15
00:01:27,460 --> 00:01:33,040
丹非常沉稳、严肃，而我是个未来的喜剧编剧。
Dan was very somber, very serious, and I was a future comedy writer.

16
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:36,960
我们的下一位嘉宾曾为 Saturday Night Live、Pixar 和 The Simpsons 写过剧本。
Our next guest has written for Saturday Night Live, Pixar, The Simpsons.

17
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:39,780
请欢迎我的好朋友西蒙·里奇回到节目中，各位。
Please welcome back to the show my very good friend Simon Rich, everybody.

18
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:43,920
我收藏了他很多很小时候写的作品。
I saved a lot of his writing from when he was really young.

19
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:47,380
我大约每隔一年就会重读一遍。
I reread them every year or so.

20
00:01:47,662 --> 00:01:50,880
作为个孩子，他的作品非常出色。
Really incredible work as a very young kid.

21
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:52,140
我们
We

22
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:57,180
上了不同的高中，我的推测是丹在社交方面可能运气不太好。
went to different high schools, and my theory is that maybe Dan didn't have great luck socially.

23
00:01:58,020 --> 00:02:01,180
他最终加入了我们的社交圈子，我很喜欢这一点。
He ended up joining our social scene, which I loved.

24
00:02:02,140 --> 00:02:05,800
有一年，他甚至出现在我们的毕业纪念册合影里。
One year, he actually posed with us in our yearbook photograph.

25
00:02:07,140 --> 00:02:12,260
大家都知道丹非常有天赋，但他的兴趣爱好非常广泛。
Everybody knew that Dan was profoundly gifted, but his interests were pretty diverse.

26
00:02:12,686 --> 00:02:21,668
有几个月时间，他痴迷于私人调查、监视，我想就是他所谓的“秘密行动”。
For several months, he was obsessed with private investigation, surveillance, I guess what he would call black ops.

27
00:02:22,094 --> 00:02:25,420
我很确定他在高中解开了一个纯数学定理。
I'm pretty sure he solved a pure math theorem in high school.

28
00:02:27,470 --> 00:02:28,550
然后他就开始钻研计算机了。
And then he got into computers.

29
00:02:29,806 --> 00:02:33,570
我在 Barnes and Noble Union Square 书店里随便打发时间。
I was randomly killing time in Barnes and Noble Union Square.

30
00:02:34,170 --> 00:02:36,610
我看到一本书叫《The Singularity is Near》。
I see a book called The Singularity is Near.

31
00:02:37,390 --> 00:02:49,550
书里他特别强调，艺术是人类最巅峰的产物，而有
了技术和人工智能，我们可以创作出更好的艺术。
And in the book, he really stresses that art is a production of humanity at
its best, and that with technology and with AI, we can produce better art.

32
00:02:49,550 --> 00:02:52,970
也就是说，我们可以更深刻地欣赏艺术，创造出更多的美。
that we can appreciate art more, we can make more beauty.

33
00:02:54,126 --> 00:02:59,690
听起来有点傻，但我瞬间就知道我要把一生都奉献给这个积极的愿景。
Sounds silly, but I knew instantly that I would devote my life to bringing about that positive vision.

34
00:03:00,782 --> 00:03:02,290
所以从一开始就和艺术有关？
So it had to do with art from the beginning?

35
00:03:03,534 --> 00:03:04,150
很大一部分是相关的。
A lot of it.

36
00:03:05,590 --> 00:03:11,610
我的意思是，还有一种更宏大的愿景，认为这是我们的宇宙宿命。
I mean, there was also the broader vision of it being our cosmic destiny.

37
00:03:12,590 --> 00:03:16,390
对我来说，这只是我众多爱好中的最新一个。
To me, this is just the latest in a long chain of hobbies.

38
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:21,670
而且这些内容非常复杂且令人困惑，完全超出了我的理解范围。
And it's also very complex and confusing to hear about, way over my head.

39
00:03:22,318 --> 00:03:23,670
大家好，谢谢大家今天来。
Hi everybody, thanks for coming today.

40
00:03:24,494 --> 00:03:29,170
我将展示一种利用形式数学来开发无缺陷机器学习系统的新方法。
So I'll be presenting a new way to develop bug-free machine learning systems using formal mathematics.

41
00:03:29,570 --> 00:03:34,590
然后你会以那个作为均值，在周围进行高斯采样，回到原始的高维空间。
And you're going to sample a Gaussian around that as the mean, back to the original high-dimensional space.

42
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:42,390
他开始告诉我奇点即将到来，我们都需要为新世界秩序做准备，
这是从我们的物种到新物种的接力传递。
He starts telling me that the singularity is coming, we all need to prepare for a new world order,
a passing of the torch from our species to a new one.

43
00:03:44,810 --> 00:03:49,290
我记得当时就在想，他是不是精神有点问题。
I do remember thinking that he maybe had mental illness at this point.

44
00:03:49,490 --> 00:03:53,330
我记得当时在想，他是不是疯了。
I do remember thinking that it was possible he had lost his mind.

45
00:03:53,930 --> 00:04:05,750
书里他著名的预测是，机器将在2029年达到人类水平，到2045年，世界将变得面目全非。
In the book, he famously predicts that machines would be human-level in 2029, and by 2045, the world would be unrecognizable.

46
00:04:05,930 --> 00:04:08,610
人类将变成超人类或后人类。
Humans will be trans or post-humans.

47
00:04:08,654 --> 00:04:16,630
世界的发展速度会快到离谱，以至于未经增强的人类甚至无法理解正在发生的一切。
the world will be going so quickly and so wildly that unenhanced humans won't even be able to comprehend what's happening.

48
00:04:17,350 --> 00:04:25,930
显然你不知道，但我一直把这个作为一种经验法则，现在依然如此。
Obviously you don't know, but I've generally anchored in that as a heuristic, and I still do.

49
00:04:26,550 --> 00:04:41,870
这种后人类奇点的愿景真的很可怕，而且很难去相信一个和你一起长大、深爱并视为挚友的人，
竟然想要去实现这样一个版本的世界。
The vision of sort of post-human singularity is really terrifying and kind of hard to believe that someone you grew up with and love
and is a close friend of yours wants to bring about this version of the world.

50
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:53,120
我的婚礼就在这儿，在……
I had my wedding here on, um...

51
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:54,820
我得确认一下。
I need to confirm this.

52
00:04:55,420 --> 00:04:56,100
那是……
It's...

53
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:57,160
M？
M?

54
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:06,140
我们聚在了一起，穿着礼服，就在乔什家人的农场上。
We're all back together, wearing our tuxes at Josh's family farm.

55
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:09,160
在纽约州极其美丽的北部地区。
Incredibly beautiful upstate New York.

56
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:09,940
我
I

57
00:05:09,940 --> 00:05:14,880
记得那天早上，我正焦头烂额地想办法系上领结。
remember that morning frantically trying to figure out how to put a bowtie on.

58
00:05:15,160 --> 00:05:17,460
丹插了一句，他说：我们可以晚点再弄那个。
Dan sort of interrupts us and he's like, we can do that later.

59
00:05:17,880 --> 00:05:20,100
我有件非常重要的事情要给你们看看。
I have something really important to show you guys.

60
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:30,760
我们就在想，这事儿难道比乔什系领结、或者比我们全员为一两个小时后的婚礼着装还要重要吗？
We're like, okay, is it more important than, you know, Josh getting his bowtie on
than us all getting dressed for Josh's wedding, which is happening in an hour or two?

61
00:05:31,020 --> 00:05:37,560
Dan 似乎完全没把婚礼的紧迫性放在心上，一心只想让我们看看他电脑里的东西。
Dan seems oblivious to the demands of the wedding and focused entirely on showing us what is on his computer.

62
00:05:38,158 --> 00:05:44,260
Dan 的眼神偶尔会变得非常深邃，用「强烈」这个词都不足以形容，我更倾向于用「预言家般的」。
Dan occasionally will get a look in his eyes that, intense isn't a strong enough word, I would describe it as prophetic.

63
00:05:45,220 --> 00:05:50,840
你、我，甚至还有即将结婚的 Josh 都说：行吧，到底是什么？
You, me, and even Josh, who was about to be married, said, okay, fine, what is it?

64
00:05:51,470 --> 00:05:54,760
我们走到 Dan 的房间，他拿出了笔记本电脑。
We went up to Dan's room where he pulled out his laptop.

65
00:05:55,790 --> 00:06:03,520
我不确定自己原本期待看到什么，但那只是一个简单的黑色窗口，左上角有个光标在闪烁。
I don't know exactly what I was expecting to see, but it was just a simple black box with a cursor in the upper left-hand corner.

66
00:06:04,078 --> 00:06:11,280
然后他基本上就说：好，你们想让它做什么？它可以用任何人的声音写任何东西，而且……
And basically said, Okay, what do you guys want to what do you guys want it to do can write anything in any voice and

67
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:12,420
不知出于什么原因，我们的……
for some reason our?

68
00:06:12,600 --> 00:06:13,900
脑子里立刻跳到了诗歌上；
Minds went straight to poetry;

69
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:19,920
我们都有同样的冲动，如果能写出一首诗，就能证明 Dan 不是在胡思乱想。
all of us had this sort of same impulse, if we can write a poem that would prove that Dan wasn't just delusional.

70
00:06:20,014 --> 00:06:21,020
然后……
And

71
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:25,340
我们就说：『要不写一首菲利普·拉金的诗？』于是……
so we're like, "How about a Philip, Philip Larkin poem ?" And

72
00:06:25,340 --> 00:06:38,340
他输入了一句提示词，就像我们现在所熟知的那个概念。它吐出了一首非常符合菲利普·拉金风格
的诗，以至于我们以为就是他写的。
he typed in a sentence prompt as we now know it to be called, and it spat out a poem,
that was so much in the style of Philip Larkin that we thought it was a poem by Philip Larkin.

73
00:06:38,400 --> 00:06:38,940
它非得是那样不可。
It had to be.

74
00:06:39,086 --> 00:06:39,920
听起来就像是拉金写的。
It sounded like Larkin.

75
00:06:40,270 --> 00:06:44,804
它有同样的机智，同样忧郁的基调，还有那种……厌世的锋芒。
It had the same wit, the same melancholy, the same, like, misanthropic edge.

76
00:06:47,246 --> 00:06:48,036
我们尖叫起来。
We screamed.

77
00:06:48,686 --> 00:06:50,244
真的，因为震惊而尖叫出来。
Like, literally screamed in shock.

78
00:06:51,310 --> 00:06:53,020
我当时脑子里没多少余力去思考其他事。
I didn't have a whole lot of mental space.

79
00:06:53,358 --> 00:07:00,100
我马上就要结婚了，但我确实记得那种感觉，就像是目睹了极其重要的时刻。
I'm about to get married, but I do remember feeling like I had seen something monumentally important.

80
00:07:01,102 --> 00:07:03,280
他说这将迎来指数级的增长。
He says that there's going to be exponential growth.

81
00:07:03,822 --> 00:07:09,640
他告诉我们，在一年之内，它会比全国任何一个高中生都要聪明。
He tells us that within a year, it's going to be smarter than every high school student in the country.

82
00:07:09,820 --> 00:07:15,260
他认为在五年或十年内，它可能会变成一种有自我意识的超级智能。
He thinks within five or ten years, it might be a sentient superintelligence.

83
00:07:19,310 --> 00:07:24,132
我们瞬间意识到世界已经变了，再也回不到从前了。
We instantly seemed to know that the world had changed and would never be the same.

84
00:07:27,150 --> 00:07:35,620
而艾米莉和乔舒亚选择分享以爱、同情和庆祝为核心的祝福。
And Emily and Joshua have chosen to share blessings centering on love, compassion, and celebration.

85
00:07:38,446 --> 00:07:39,020
我们……
We

86
00:07:45,520 --> 00:07:55,200
我们挨个儿去告诉他们发生了什么，包括乔什，尽管他当时有更重要的任务要处理。
went around, all of us, including Josh, even though he had a far more important obligation, and tried to tell them what was happening.

87
00:07:55,820 --> 00:08:05,440
但人们的反应却是完全的沉默和漠不关心。
And people's reaction was absolute silence and disinterest.

88
00:08:06,894 --> 00:08:17,980
我记得 Dan 站在农舍的大厅里，他不知怎么就抓住了我的伴娘，而我正需要她帮我个忙，
可他一直在滔滔不绝地讲：嗯，要是艾米莉·狄金森写一首关于痔疮膏的诗会怎么样？
I remember Dan standing in the foyer of the farmhouse, and he had just like somehow gotten hold of my maid of honor,
and I needed her for something, and he just kept talking about, well,

89
00:08:17,980 --> 00:08:22,100
我记得 Dan 站在农舍的大厅里，他不知怎么就抓住了我的伴娘，而我正需要她帮我个忙，
可他一直在滔滔不绝地讲：嗯，要是艾米莉·狄金森写一首关于痔疮膏的诗会怎么样？
like, what if Emily Dickinson wrote a poem about hemorrhoid cream?

90
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:25,820
就像是在说：不管这事儿是什么，听起来挺奇怪。
Was just sort of like, whatever's going on here, it sounds strange.

91
00:08:25,820 --> 00:08:28,388
就像是：我们没时间管这些。
Like, we don't have time for any of this.

92
00:08:44,010 --> 00:08:57,350
这有点像有个朋友在51区工作，他借给我一个外星人，我试着把它展示给别人看，
结果大家只是看看，然后翻个白眼说你们反应过度了。
It was a little bit like having a friend who works for Area 51 and being lent the alien and trying to show it to people and everybody
kind of looking at it and then rolling their eyes and saying you guys are overreacting.

93
00:09:00,046 --> 00:09:06,950
所以我们决定尝试用一个叫 Code Da Vinci 2 的程序把这些诗出版成一本书。
So we decided we would try to release the poems as a book using the program called Code Da Vinci 2.

94
00:09:07,374 --> 00:09:12,210
那是一段非常有成效的创意头脑风暴期。
It was this really fruitful period of creative brainstorming.

95
00:09:12,878 --> 00:09:13,970
灵感迸发了。
Lightning was striking.

96
00:09:14,210 --> 00:09:16,590
每两首诗就像是杰作一样。
Every two poems was like a masterpiece.

97
00:09:18,318 --> 00:09:18,870
太不可思议了。
Incredible.

98
00:09:19,690 --> 00:09:27,330
乔什一直在不停地跟我聊这个软件想让我读的内容，你知道的，感觉像是成千上万首诗。
Josh just talking non-stop about whatever the software was wanting to read me, you know, what felt like a million poems.

99
00:09:27,330 --> 00:09:30,970
这只是在说，我丈夫现在非常痴迷于这个。
It was just sort of like, my husband is really into this right now.

100
00:09:31,510 --> 00:09:32,670
我其实并不在乎它。
I don't really care about it.

101
00:09:32,790 --> 00:09:37,190
我不想听这些诗，而且这真的让人很不方便。
I don't want to hear about these poems, and this is just incredibly inconvenient.

102
00:09:38,030 --> 00:09:47,650
我的图书编辑在确信 AI 是真实的，而不是某个前
SNL 编剧和他的朋友搞的恶作剧后，同意出版这本书。
My book editor, once he was convinced the AI was real and not a hoax
by a former SNL writer and his friends, agreed to publish the book.

103
00:09:48,174 --> 00:09:57,030
他们兴奋的一点是，这将成为一本很有趣的“奇妙组合”之书，
因为诗歌的编辑之一是我，另一个作者是这个喜剧作家。
One of the things they're excited about is that it's going to be this sort of odd couple book,
because you've got one of the editors of the poems is me, one of their authors, this comedy writer.

104
00:09:57,030 --> 00:10:00,804
另一个编辑是这位计算机科学家。
The other editor is this computer scientist.

105
00:10:01,610 --> 00:10:02,790
我当时对这些诗感到很兴奋。
I was excited about the poems.

106
00:10:03,502 --> 00:10:08,790
事实上，我们有那首莎士比亚 AI 诗，挂在我们 OpenAI 的办公室里。
Actually, the Shakespeare AI poem we have hanging in our office at OpenAI.

107
00:10:08,790 --> 00:10:10,650
它挂在 OpenAI 办公室的某个地方。
It's hanging somewhere in the OpenAI office.

108
00:10:10,690 --> 00:10:10,930
是的。
Yeah.

109
00:10:11,502 --> 00:10:12,250
它是一个
It's a

110
00:10:12,250 --> 00:10:12,990
挺漂亮的框子。
nice framing, too.

111
00:10:13,410 --> 00:10:17,390
我想我们读了超过两万首诗。
I think we read upwards of 20,000 poems.

112
00:10:19,580 --> 00:10:25,560
我们很快就意识到，这些模仿诗并不好编纂或者阅读。
We very quickly realized that these imitation poems were not very fun to compile or read.

113
00:10:25,560 --> 00:10:40,730
你有一个 AI 程序，它窃取了人类整个历史进程中的所有创造力，剽窃了这些已故的、
有时被边缘化的诗人的声音，然后吐出仿制品。
You have this AI program that has stolen all of the creativity of humans throughout the course of history and ripped off the voices of
these dead, sometimes marginalized poets and spat out counterfeits.

114
00:10:41,130 --> 00:10:51,396
它从每一个人身上学习，所以它是对过去那些做出过杰出、美丽、有趣、
美妙成就的所有人的反映和致敬。
It's learned from everybody, and so it's a reflection and it's credit to all of
the people who have done striking, beautiful, funny, beautiful things in the past.

115
00:10:51,822 --> 00:10:53,412
嗯，这是一种没有署名的致敬。
Well, it's an uncredited credit.

116
00:10:54,414 --> 00:11:00,630
我们很快就代表全人类开始讨厌这些模仿诗了。
We just very rapidly grew to hate the Imitation Pumps on behalf of humanity.

117
00:11:01,450 --> 00:11:16,970
所以我们正考虑出于道德原因放弃这个项目，这时我们产生了一个奇怪的主意：
